<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dilmun Burial Mounds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2050–1750 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> Burial Mounds are the tens of thousands of earth and stone tumuli built across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> mainly between about 2050 and 1750 BCE, in the Early Dilmun period. They range from small individual graves to large royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aali">A'ali</a>. Many have been lost to modern building, and twenty-one archaeological sites were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dilmun-burial-mounds.jpg" alt="Dilmun Burial Mounds" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Storrs-Fox, Edwin Aubrey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burial_Mounds_in_Bahrain_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Dilmun Burial Mounds are necropolis areas on the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, dating to the Dilmun and the Umm al-Nar cultures. The cemeteries lie in the north of the island, on hard stony ground slightly above the arable soils, while the south is mainly sandy desert. Bahrain has long been known as an island with a very large number of burials, and the mounds, which once covered a number of square kilometres, were said to form one of the largest cemeteries of the ancient world.</p><p>Recent studies suggest that the local population alone could have produced an estimated 350,000 ancient grave mounds over some thousands of years. The graves differ in era, style and size across the moundfield, and research under the Bahrain National Museum continues in order to fix a firm timeline and to understand the societies that built them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Each tumulus has a central stone chamber enclosed by a low ring-wall and covered with earth and gravel. Most mounds measure about 4.5 by 9 metres across and stand 1 to 2 metres high. The smaller ones usually hold a single chamber, generally rectangular with one or two alcoves at the northeast end.</p><p>The dead were usually laid on their right sides with their heads toward the alcove, accompanied by few goods: a little pottery, shell or stone stamp seals, baskets sealed with asphalt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> objects, stone jars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> weapons. The skeletons include both sexes, with a life expectancy of about 40 years, and babies were generally buried at or outside the ring-wall.</p><p>From 10 to 19 February 1889 the British explorer J. Theodore Bent and his wife Mabel excavated some of the mounds and found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and ostrich eggshell, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aali">A'ali</a>, in Mound 8, they found three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscriptions, all variants of a text naming the palace of the son of Ri'mum, servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inzak">Inzak</a> of Akarum. The same text turned up on the surface at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-al-bahrain">Qal'at al-Bahrain</a>. In the 1950s a Danish group digging at Qal'at al-Bahrain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> capital, opened tumuli with items of about 4100 to 3700 BP, and many others later excavated more graves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mounds are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Efforts to protect them have met opposition from religious fundamentalists who regard them as un-Islamic and have called for them to be built over with housing. In a parliamentary debate on 17 July 2005 Sheikh Adel Mouwda, leader of the Salafist al Asalah party, argued that housing for the living was better than graves for the dead.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dilmun Burial Mounds” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qal’at al-Bahrain became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005 and the <b>Dilmun Burial Mounds</b> followed in 2019, and Dilmun is today a central part of Bahrain’s national identity and heritage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
